          "content": "## Root Cause Analysis Complete\n\nThe error `-32602: Invalid request parameters(\"\")` is caused by the **MCP client sending `arguments: \"\"` (empty string) instead of `arguments: {}` (empty object)**.\n\nThis is a **client-side serialization issue**, not a server-side problem. The MCP SDK's Pydantic validation correctly rejects the empty string because `arguments` must be a dictionary.\n\n### Verification\n\n```python\n# This FAILS with -32602\n{\"method\": \"tools/call\", \"params\": {\"name\": \"get_toolpath_details\", \"arguments\": \"\"}}\n\n# This WORKS\n{\"method\": \"tools/call\", \"params\": {\"name\": \"get_toolpath_details\", \"arguments\": {}}}\n\n# This also WORKS (arguments omitted)\n{\"method\": \"tools/call\", \"params\": {\"name\": \"get_toolpath_details\"}}\n```\n\n### Why ~60% of Tools Fail\n\nThe pattern is clear:\n- **Tools WITHOUT parameters** (13 tools): These likely work because the client might not send `arguments` at all\n- **Tools WITH required parameters** (51 tools): These fail when the client sends `arguments: \"\"`\n\n### The Fix\n\nSince this is a client-side issue (Kiro CLI or whatever MCP client is being used), there are two approaches:\n\n**Option 1: Fix the client** (preferred)\nThe MCP client should send `arguments: {}` or omit `arguments` entirely when there are no arguments, not `arguments: \"\"`.\n\n**Option 2: Server-side workaround**\nAdd a request interceptor to convert empty string arguments to empty dict before validation.\n\nLet me create a server-side workaround since you may not have control over the client:",
