                    "Text": "\"\"\",\n)\n`\nThe `FastMCP` constructor accepts several arguments:\n\n## FastMCP Constructor Parameters\n\n[​][42]\nname\nstr\ndefault:\"FastMCP\"\nA human-readable name for your server\n[​][43]\ninstructions\nstr | None\nDescription of how to interact with this server. These instructions help clients understand the server’s purpose and available functionality\n[​][44]\nversion\nstr | None\nVersion string for your server. If not provided, defaults to the FastMCP library version\n[​][45]\nwebsite_url\nstr | None\nNew in version `2. 13. 0`URL to a website with more information about your server. Displayed in client applications\n[​][46]\nicons\nlist[Icon] | None\nNew in version `2. 13. 0`List of icon representations for your server. Icons help users visually identify your server in client applications. See [Icons][47] for detailed examples\n[​][48]\nauth\nOAuthProvider | TokenVerifier | None\nAuthentication provider for securing HTTP-based transports. See [Authentication][49] for configuration options\n[​][50]\nlifespan\nAsyncContextManager | None\nAn async context manager function for server startup and shutdown logic\n[​][51]\ntools\nlist[Tool | Callable] | None\nA list of tools (or functions to convert to tools) to add to the server. In some cases, providing tools programmatically may be more convenient than using the `@mcp. tool` decorator\n[​][52]\ninclude_tags\nset[str] | None\nOnly expose components with at least one matching tag\n[​][53]\nexclude_tags\nset[str] | None\nHide components with any matching tag\n[​][54]\non_duplicate_tools\nLiteral[\"error\", \"warn\", \"replace\"]\ndefault:\"error\"\nHow to handle duplicate tool registrations\n[​][55]\non_duplicate_resources\nLiteral[\"error\", \"warn\", \"replace\"]\ndefault:\"warn\"\nHow to handle duplicate resource registrations\n[​][56]\non_duplicate_prompts\nLiteral[\"error\", \"warn\", \"replace\"]\ndefault:\"replace\"\nHow to handle duplicate prompt registrations\n[​][57]\nstrict_input_validation\nbool\ndefault:\"False\"\nNew in version `2. 13. 0`Controls how tool input parameters are validated. When `False` (default), FastMCP uses Pydantic’s flexible validation that coerces compatible inputs (e. g. , `\"10\"` → `10` for int parameters). When `True`, uses the MCP SDK’s JSON Schema validation to validate inputs against the exact schema before passing them to your function, rejecting any type mismatches. The default mode improves compatibility with LLM clients while maintaining type safety. See [Input Validation Modes][58] for details\n[​][59]\ninclude_fastmcp_meta\nbool\ndefault:\"True\"\nNew in version `2. This allows you to instantly convert existing APIs into MCP servers without manual tool creation. See the [FastAPI Integration][79] and [OpenAPI Integration][80] guides for detailed examples and configuration options. Copy\n`import httpx\nfrom fastmcp import FastMCP\n\n# From OpenAPI spec\nspec = httpx. get(\"https://api. example. com/openapi. json\"). json()\nmcp = FastMCP. from_openapi(openapi_spec=spec, client=httpx. AsyncClient())\n\n# From FastAPI app\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\napp = FastAPI()\nmcp = FastMCP. from_fastapi(app=app)\n`\n\n## [​][81]\n## Server Configuration\n\nServers can be configured using a combination of initialization arguments, global settings, and transport-specific settings. ### [​][82]\n### Server-Specific Configuration\n\nServer-specific settings are passed when creating the `FastMCP` instance and control server behavior:\nCopy\n`from fastmcp import FastMCP\n\n# Configure server-specific settings\nmcp = FastMCP(\n    name=\"ConfiguredServer\",\n    include_tags={\"public\", \"api\"},              # Only expose these tagged components\n    exclude_tags={\"internal\", \"deprecated\"},     # Hide these tagged components\n    on_duplicate_tools=\"error\",                  # Handle duplicate registrations\n    on_duplicate_resources=\"warn\",\n    on_duplicate_prompts=\"replace\",\n    include_fastmcp_meta=False,                  # Disable FastMCP metadata for cleaner integration\n)\n`\n\n### [​][83]\n### Global Settings\n\nGlobal settings affect all FastMCP servers and can be configured via environment variables (prefixed with `FASTMCP_`) or in a `. env` file:\nCopy\n`import fastmcp\n\n# Access global settings\nprint(fastmcp. settings. log_level)        # Default: \"INFO\"\nprint(fastmcp. settings. mask_error_details)  # Default: False\nprint(fastmcp. settings. strict_input_validation)  # Default: False\nprint(fastmcp. settings. include_fastmcp_meta)   # Default: True\n`\nCommon global settings include:\n\n* **`log_level`**: Logging level (“DEBUG”, “INFO”, “WARNING”, “ERROR”, “CRITICAL”), set with `FASTMCP_LOG_LEVEL`\n* **`mask_error_details`**: Whether to hide detailed error information from clients, set with `FASTMCP_MASK_ERROR_DETAILS`\n* **`strict_input_validation`**: Controls tool input validation mode (default: False for flexible coercion), set with `FASTMCP_STRICT_INPUT_VALIDATION`. 2. 7` By default, FastMCP serializes tool return values to JSON when they need to be converted to text. You can customize this behavior by providing a `tool_serializer` function when creating your server:\nCopy\n`import yaml\nfrom fastmcp import FastMCP\n\n# Define a custom serializer that formats dictionaries as YAML\ndef yaml_serializer(data):\n    return yaml. dump(data, sort_keys=False)\n\n# Create a server with the custom serializer\nmcp = FastMCP(name=\"MyServer\", tool_serializer=yaml_serializer)\n\n@mcp. tool\ndef get_config():\n    \"\"\"Returns configuration in YAML format. \"\"\"\n    return {\"api_key\": \"abc123\", \"debug\": True, \"rate_limit\": 100}\n`\nThe serializer function takes any data object and returns a string representation. This is applied to **all non-string return values** from your tools. Tools that already return strings bypass the serializer. This customization is useful when you want to:\n\n* Format data in a specific way (like YAML or custom formats)\n* Control specific serialization options (like indentation or sorting)\n* Add metadata or transform data before sending it to clients\nIf the serializer function raises an exception, the tool will fall back to the default JSON serialization to avoid breaking the server. [\nFastMCP Updates\nPrevious\n][88][\nTools\nNext\n][89]\n⌘I\n[discord][90][github][91][website][92][x][93]\n[Powered by][94]\n\n[1]: #content-area\n[2]: https://pyai. events?utm_source=gofastmcp\n[3]: /\n[4]: https://fastmcp. cloud\n[5]: https://discord. gg/uu8dJCgttd\n[6]: https://github. com/jlowin/fastmcp\n[7]: https://github. com/jlowin/fastmcp\n[8]: /getting-started/welcome\n[9]: /getting-started/installation\n[10]: /getting-started/quickstart\n[11]: /updates\n[12]: /servers/server\n[13]: /patterns/tool-transformation\n[14]: /patterns/decorating-methods\n[15]: /patterns/cli\n[16]: /patterns/contrib\n[17]: /patterns/testing\n[18]: /development/contributing\n[19]: /development/tests\n[20]: /development/releases\n[21]: /development/upgrade-guide\n[22]: /changelog\n[23]: #creating-a-server\n[24]: #components\n[25]: #tools\n[26]: #resources\n[27]: #resource-templates\n[28]: #prompts\n[29]: #tag-based-filtering\n[30]: #running-the-server\n[31]: #custom-routes\n[32]: #composing-servers\n[33]: #proxying-servers\n[34]: #openapi-integration\n[35]: #server-configuration\n[36]: #server-specific-configuration\n[37]: #global-settings\n[38]: #transport-specific-configuration\n[39]: #setting-global-configuration\n[40]: #custom-tool-serialization\n[41]: #creating-a-server\n[42]: #param-name\n[43]: #param-instructions\n[44]: #param-version\n[45]: #param-website-url\n[46]: #param-icons\n[47]: /servers/icons\n[48]: #param-auth\n[49]: /servers/auth/authentication\n[50]: #param-lifespan\n[51]: #param-tools\n[52]: #param-include-tags\n[53]: #param-exclude-tags\n[54]: #param-on-duplicate-tools\n[55]: #param-on-duplicate-resources\n[56]: #param-on-duplicate-prompts\n[57]: #param-strict-input-validation\n[58]: /server